open scoped WeierstrassCurve.Affine in
/-- **GALOIS DESCENT FOR THE AFFINE POINTS OF A WEIERSTRASS CURVE OVER `ℚ`**
(PROVEN 2026-07-31, flt-lean-49; no leaf): a `ℚ̄`-point of `E` fixed by the
whole absolute Galois group is the base change of a `ℚ`-point.

This is `E(ℚ) = E(ℚ̄)^{Γ_ℚ}`, in the only direction that is not free, and it
is the Weierstrass-side twin of `Fermat.exists_ratPoint_of_galoisInvariant`
(the scheme-side statement, PROVEN in `X0.lean`).

THE PROOF, and it is two lines of mathematics.  A point is `0` — which
descends trivially — or `some x y h`, and `Point.map σ` is `some (σ x) (σ y)`,
so fixedness is exactly `σ x = x` and `σ y = y` for every `σ`.
`Fermat.mem_range_algebraMap_of_galoisFixed` (PROVEN in `X0.lean`, over
`InfiniteGalois.mem_range_algebraMap_iff_fixed`; stated over a VARIABLE
perfect field on purpose, see its docstring for why the literal `ℚ` breaks
instance synthesis) puts both coordinates in `ℚ`, and
`WeierstrassCurve.Affine.baseChange_nonsingular` — which is an `↔` along any
INJECTIVE algebra map — brings the nonsingularity back down with them.

**Not vacuous**: the hypothesis holds for every point in the image of
`Point.map (Algebra.ofId ℚ ℚ̄)`, by `Point.map_baseChange`, and that image is
nonempty (it contains `0`).  **`hP` is load-bearing**: without it the
statement is false for any `E` with an irrational point, e.g. `E : y² = x³ + 2`
and the point `(∛2, 2)` over `ℚ̄`, whose `x` is not in `ℚ`. -/
theorem exists_point_map_eq_of_galoisFixed (E : WeierstrassCurve ℚ)
    (P : (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).Point)
    (hP : ∀ σ : Field.absoluteGaloisGroup ℚ,
      WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.map
        (σ : AlgebraicClosure ℚ ≃ₐ[ℚ] AlgebraicClosure ℚ).toAlgHom P = P) :
    ∃ Q : E.toAffine.Point,
      WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.map (W' := E.toAffine)
        (Algebra.ofId ℚ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)) Q = P := by
  cases P with
  | zero => exact ⟨0, rfl⟩
  | some x y h =>
    have hx : ∀ σ : Field.absoluteGaloisGroup ℚ,
        (σ : AlgebraicClosure ℚ ≃ₐ[ℚ] AlgebraicClosure ℚ) x = x := by
      intro σ
      have hσ := hP σ
      rw [WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.map_some] at hσ
      exact (WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.some.inj hσ).1
    have hy : ∀ σ : Field.absoluteGaloisGroup ℚ,
        (σ : AlgebraicClosure ℚ ≃ₐ[ℚ] AlgebraicClosure ℚ) y = y := by
      intro σ
      have hσ := hP σ
      rw [WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.map_some] at hσ
      exact (WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.some.inj hσ).2
    obtain ⟨x₀, hx₀⟩ := Fermat.mem_range_algebraMap_of_galoisFixed (K := ℚ) x hx
    obtain ⟨y₀, hy₀⟩ := Fermat.mem_range_algebraMap_of_galoisFixed (K := ℚ) y hy
    subst hx₀
    subst hy₀
    have h₀ : (E.toAffine⁄ℚ).Nonsingular x₀ y₀ :=
      (WeierstrassCurve.Affine.baseChange_nonsingular (W := E.toAffine)
        (f := Algebra.ofId ℚ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))
        (Algebra.ofId ℚ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).injective x₀ y₀).mp h
    exact ⟨.some x₀ y₀ h₀, rfl⟩

open scoped WeierstrassCurve.Affine in
/-- **THE `ℚ`-POINTS OF A GENUS-ONE CURVE WITH A GROUP LAW ARE THE `ℚ`-POINTS
OF A WEIERSTRASS CURVE** (PROVEN 2026-07-31, flt-lean-49; no leaf, no level,
no modular content): `X(ℚ) ≃ E(ℚ)`, an honest BIJECTION and not merely an
injection.

This is the whole scheme-side content of
`exists_relPoint_inj_x0Model_of_abelianSchemeStruct` below, and after it that
leaf owes only the identification of `E` with `N a 1`.

THE ROUTE, four citations and no new geometry:

1. `Fermat.exists_weierstrassCurve_of_abelianSchemeStruct` produces `E`,
   `E.IsElliptic`, and a `Γ_ℚ`-equivariant `≃+` from `(E⁄ℚ̄).Point` to
   `GeomFibrePt strX (𝟙 SpecQ)`;
2. `Fermat.ratToGeom` embeds `X(ℚ)` in the geometric points
   (`Fermat.injective_ratToGeom`) with Galois-fixed image
   (`Fermat.galSMul_ratToGeom`); both PROVEN;
3. transporting along the equivariant `≃+` and descending with
   `exists_point_map_eq_of_galoisFixed` above gives the map `X(ℚ) → E(ℚ)`;
4. SURJECTIVITY is the reverse descent: `Point.map_baseChange` says the base
   change of a rational point is Galois-fixed, and
   `Fermat.exists_ratPoint_of_galoisInvariant` (PROVEN) turns the
   corresponding fixed geometric point back into a rational point of `X`.

**WHY THE BIJECTION AND NOT THE INJECTION, and this is load-bearing rather
than a flourish.**  The leaf below has to PIN `E`.  With only an injection
`X(ℚ) ↪ E(ℚ)` the hypothesis says nothing about `E` — every curve with
enough rational points satisfies it, including rank-one ones — so a residual
leaf of the form "…hence `E(ℚ) ↪ N a 1(ℚ)`" would be plainly FALSE.  The
bijection makes `#E(ℚ) = #X_0(N)(ℚ)` and the residue true.  A cut that kept
only the injection would have manufactured a false leaf; this is the
`exists_artinDivisorNormIndex_le_ray_class` failure mode (two individually
reasonable steps composing into a false statement) caught before it landed.

**`hdim` is load-bearing** and is exactly the hypothesis
`exists_weierstrassCurve_of_abelianSchemeStruct` refutes the statement
without: at relative dimension `0`, `Spec ℚ` with its trivial group law is a
proper smooth connected abelian scheme with one geometric point, while
`(E⁄ℚ̄).Point` is infinite.  At the consumer it is free — it is the `smooth`
field of `Fermat.IsX0Compactification`. -/
theorem exists_weierstrassPointEquiv_of_abelianSchemeStruct {X : Scheme.{0}}
    {strX : X ⟶ SpecQ} (ab : AbelianSchemeStruct strX)
    (hdim : SmoothOfRelativeDimension 1 strX) :
    ∃ (E : WeierstrassCurve ℚ) (_ : E.IsElliptic),
      Nonempty (RelPoint strX (𝟙 SpecQ) ≃ E.toAffine.Point) := by
  classical
  letI := ab.addCommGroup (specAlgClos ℚ ≫ 𝟙 SpecQ)
  obtain ⟨E, hE, e, he⟩ := exists_weierstrassCurve_of_abelianSchemeStruct ab hdim
  -- every rational point of `X` becomes a Galois-fixed point of `(E⁄ℚ̄)`, hence descends
  have key : ∀ P : RelPoint strX (𝟙 SpecQ),
      ∃ Q : E.toAffine.Point,
        WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.map (W' := E.toAffine)
            (Algebra.ofId ℚ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)) Q
          = e.symm (ratToGeom strX P) := by
    intro P
    refine exists_point_map_eq_of_galoisFixed E _ ?_
    intro σ
    apply e.injective
    rw [he σ (e.symm (ratToGeom strX P)), e.apply_symm_apply, galSMul_ratToGeom ab σ P]
  choose g hg using key
  refine ⟨E, hE, ⟨Equiv.ofBijective g ⟨?_, ?_⟩⟩⟩
  · -- injectivity: `Point.map`, `e.symm` and `ratToGeom` are all injective
    intro P P' hPP'
    apply injective_ratToGeom strX
    apply e.symm.injective
    rw [← hg P, ← hg P', hPP']
  · -- surjectivity: the reverse descent, on the scheme side this time
    intro Q
    set x : (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).Point :=
      WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.map (W' := E.toAffine)
        (Algebra.ofId ℚ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)) Q with hxdef
    have hxfix : ∀ σ : Field.absoluteGaloisGroup ℚ,
        WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.map
          (σ : AlgebraicClosure ℚ ≃ₐ[ℚ] AlgebraicClosure ℚ).toAlgHom x = x := by
      intro σ
      exact WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.map_baseChange (W' := E.toAffine)
        (F := ℚ) (K := AlgebraicClosure ℚ) (L := AlgebraicClosure ℚ)
        (σ : AlgebraicClosure ℚ ≃ₐ[ℚ] AlgebraicClosure ℚ).toAlgHom Q
    have hy : ∀ σ : Field.absoluteGaloisGroup ℚ,
        ab.galSMul (𝟙 SpecQ) σ (e x) = e x := by
      intro σ
      rw [← he σ x, hxfix σ]
    obtain ⟨P, hP⟩ := exists_ratPoint_of_galoisInvariant ab (e x) hy
    refine ⟨P, ?_⟩
    apply WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.map_injective
      (W' := E.toAffine) (f := Algebra.ofId ℚ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))
    rw [hg P, hP, e.symm_apply_apply]

/-- **WHICH ELLIPTIC CURVE `X_0(N)` IS, AT THE FOUR GENUS-ONE LEVELS** (sorry
leaf, cut 2026-07-31 (flt-lean-49) out of
