/-- **Every `modelTable` row has a banked charpoly row, whose trace column is
`ℓ + 1 − m`** (PROVEN 2026-07-30, one `decide` and one `norm_num` per row) — the
exact analogue of `Fermat.exists_charpolyRow_of_x0WitnessTable`, and the drift
guard between `modelTable` and `Fermat.x0HeckeCharpolyTable`.

At all four levels `dim_ℂ S₂(Γ₀(N)) = 1`, so `charpoly(T_ℓ) = X − a_ℓ` and the
stored coefficient list is `[−a_ℓ, 1]`.  Reading the trace off it as
`−c.getD (d−1) 0 = −c.getD 0 0 = a_ℓ` and comparing with `ℓ + 1 − m`:

| `N` | `ℓ` | `c` | `a_ℓ` | `ℓ + 1 − m` |
|-----|-----|-----|-------|--------------|
| `11` | `3` | `[1, 1]`  | `−1` | `4 − 5 = −1` |
| `17` | `3` | `[0, 1]`  | `0`  | `4 − 4 = 0`  |
| `19` | `5` | `[-3, 1]` | `3`  | `6 − 3 = 3`  |
| `32` | `3` | `[0, 1]`  | `0`  | `4 − 4 = 0`  |

A row deleted from or mistyped in `x0HeckeCharpolyTable`, and equally a wrong
`m` or a wrong witness prime in `modelTable`, breaks HERE at a `decide` rather
than silently downstream.  That is the whole reason this lemma exists as a
separate step instead of being inlined. -/
theorem exists_charpolyRow_of_modelTable {N ℓ m : ℕ} {a₁ a₂ a₃ a₄ a₆ : ℤ}
    (h : (N, ℓ, a₁, a₂, a₃, a₄, a₆, m) ∈ modelTable) :
    ∃ (d : ℕ) (c : List ℤ), 0 < d ∧ (N, ℓ, d, c) ∈ x0HeckeCharpolyTable ∧
      -((c.getD (d - 1) 0 : ℤ) : ℂ) = (ℓ : ℂ) + 1 - (m : ℂ) := by
  fin_cases h
  · exact ⟨1, [1, 1], one_pos, by decide, by norm_num⟩
  · exact ⟨1, [0, 1], one_pos, by decide, by norm_num⟩
  · exact ⟨1, [-3, 1], one_pos, by decide, by norm_num⟩
  · exact ⟨1, [0, 1], one_pos, by decide, by norm_num⟩

/-- **`Tr(T_ℓ ∣ S₂(Γ₀(N))) = ℓ + 1 − m` at the four genus-one rows** (PROVEN
2026-07-30) — the `modelTable` analogue of
`Fermat.traceHeckeOp_of_x0WitnessTable`, and the arithmetic half of the point
count.

Its whole proof is `Fermat.trace_heckeOp_of_charpolyTable` composed with the
row-matching lemma above, so no dimension hypothesis and no basis appear here:
`LinearMap.trace` is TOTAL, and the finite-dimensionality of
`CuspForm (Gamma0GL N) 2` — which is `cuspForm_finiteDimensional` and lives
DOWNSTREAM of this file in `Modularity/Interface.lean`, hence is unavailable
here — is consumed inside `Fermat.exists_basis_charpoly_heckeOp` in `X0.lean`,
where it is derived from that table's dimension leaf instead.  That is why the
count can be assembled in this module at all. -/
theorem traceHeckeOp_of_modelTable {N ℓ m : ℕ} {a₁ a₂ a₃ a₄ a₆ : ℤ}
    (h : (N, ℓ, a₁, a₂, a₃, a₄, a₆, m) ∈ modelTable) :
    LinearMap.trace ℂ
        (CuspForm (_root_.GaloisRepresentation.Modularity.Gamma0GL N) 2)
        (_root_.GaloisRepresentation.Modularity.heckeOp N ℓ)
      = (ℓ : ℂ) + 1 - (m : ℂ) := by
  obtain ⟨d, c, hd, hrow, hval⟩ := exists_charpolyRow_of_modelTable h
  rw [trace_heckeOp_of_charpolyTable hd hrow, hval]

/-- **`m` is positive at every `modelTable` row** (PROVEN, `decide` per row) —
`5, 4, 3, 4`.  Small, and load-bearing twice below: it is what turns the count
into a NONEMPTY point set, and what supplies the `Finite` instances (over a
possibly-infinite type `Nat.card` is `0`, so `Nat.card = m` with `0 < m` is
strictly more than a cardinality claim). -/
theorem pos_of_mem_modelTable {N ℓ m : ℕ} {a₁ a₂ a₃ a₄ a₆ : ℤ}
    (h : (N, ℓ, a₁, a₂, a₃, a₄, a₆, m) ∈ modelTable) : 0 < m := by
  fin_cases h <;> norm_num

/-- **EICHLER–SHIMURA AT THE FOUR GENUS-ONE ROWS: `#X_0(N)(𝔽_ℓ) = m`** (PROVEN
2026-07-30) — the `modelTable` analogue of `Fermat.card_relPoint_x0_finiteField`,
whose eleven rows are `Fermat.x0WitnessTable`, and the single statement that the
two former `sorry` leaves below now rest on.

The assembly is the same bookkeeping as its `x0WitnessTable` twin: the geometric
input gives the count as `ℓ + 1 − Tr T_ℓ` in `ℂ`
(`Fermat.card_relPoint_x0_eichlerShimura`, whose three hypotheses come from
`modelTable_spec` and one `norm_num`), the arithmetic input evaluates the trace
(`traceHeckeOp_of_modelTable`), and `ℕ → ℂ` is injective.

**Quantified over EVERY compactification**, exactly as its twin is, and safe for
the same reason: `card_relPoint_x0_eichlerShimura` is, and `IsX0Compactification`
pins `(X, Y, jY)` up to isomorphism anyway.  So no existential-then-transport
step is needed here, which is what makes the two leaves below collapse to
bookkeeping.

**NOT VACUOUS**: `Fermat.exists_x0Compactification_finiteField` supplies `hX` at
every row (its three hypotheses are `modelTable_spec`'s), and `0 < m`, so this
asserts a specific nonzero count of a set that could a priori be empty. -/
theorem card_relPoint_of_modelTable {N ℓ m : ℕ} {a₁ a₂ a₃ a₄ a₆ : ℤ}
    (h : (N, ℓ, a₁, a₂, a₃, a₄, a₆, m) ∈ modelTable)
    {X Y : Scheme.{0}} {strX : X ⟶ SpecF ℓ} {strY : Y ⟶ SpecF ℓ} {jY : Y ⟶ X}
    (hX : IsX0Compactification N strX strY jY) :
    Nat.card (RelPoint strX (𝟙 (SpecF ℓ))) = m := by
  obtain ⟨hNlev, hℓp, hℓN, -⟩ := modelTable_spec h
  have hNpos : 0 < N := by fin_cases hNlev <;> norm_num
  have h1 := card_relPoint_x0_eichlerShimura N ℓ hNpos hℓp hℓN hX
  rw [traceHeckeOp_of_modelTable h] at h1
  have h2 : ((Nat.card (RelPoint strX (𝟙 (SpecF ℓ))) : ℕ) : ℂ) = (m : ℂ) := by
    rw [h1]; ring
  exact_mod_cast h2

