**NEW, 2026-07-30 — the `coarse.classify` route is not merely long, it is
FALSE.**  It is the most tempting route of all and the audit does not mention
it: `IsX0Compactification.coarse.classify (𝟙 _)` sends a `Gamma0Datum N (SpecF ℓ)`
to a point of `Y_0(N)_{𝔽_ℓ}`, and composing with `jY` gives exactly the point
this statement asks for.  So the leaf would reduce to
`Nonempty (Gamma0Datum N (SpecF ℓ))` — an elliptic curve over `𝔽_ℓ` with a
Frobenius-stable cyclic subgroup scheme of order `N`.  **That reduction target is
false at the row `(32, 3)`**, so a successor who took this route would
manufacture a false leaf out of a true one.

Counterexample, and it is a finite computation.  `3 ∤ 32`, so `E[32]` is étale
and a cyclic subgroup scheme of order `32` over `𝔽_3` is a `π`-stable cyclic
`ℤ/32`-submodule of `E[32](𝔽̄_3) ≅ (ℤ/32)²`, where `π` is Frobenius.  A generator
`v` of such a submodule satisfies `π v = λ v`, and `π² − aπ + 3 = 0` then forces
`λ² − aλ + 3 ≡ 0 (mod 32)`.  For `E/𝔽_3` the trace satisfies `|a| ≤ 2√3 < 4`, so
`a ∈ {−3, …, 3}`; PARI/GP enumerating all `32` residues at each of those seven
`a` finds **no** root — already none mod `8` — so no such `E` exists and
`Y_0(32)(𝔽_3)` is empty.  Consistently, `#X_0(32)(𝔽_3) = 4` by
`card_relPoint_of_modelTable`, and all four of those points are cusps.  (Untrusted
searcher; but the argument above is a two-line kernel computation if anyone wants
it, and the conclusion is only used to CLOSE a route, never to open one.)

Moral, and it generalises past this leaf: over a finite field the compactified
curve can have points while the open modular part has none, so `X_0` and `Y_0`
must not be interchanged in a rational-point argument — which is precisely the
asymmetry `Fermat.y0HasNoRationalPoint_of_cuspidal` exploits over `ℚ`.

