**RETIRED AUDIT (2026-07-28/30), kept verbatim below this line.**

**TRUE, and by the Weil bound alone.**  `IsX0Compactification` makes `strX`
proper (`isProper`), smooth of relative dimension `1` (`smooth`) and
geometrically connected (`connected`) over `Spec 𝔽_ℓ`, and at `N ∈ levels`
its genus is exactly `1` (`x0Genus_eq_one`, `decide`).  A smooth proper
geometrically connected genus-`1` curve over `𝔽_q` satisfies
`#X(𝔽_q) ≥ q + 1 − 2√q = (√q − 1)² > 0` for every `q ≥ 2`, so the point set
is nonempty.  Equivalently — and this is the shape the classical literature
uses — `X` is a torsor under its Jacobian and Lang's theorem says every
torsor under a connected algebraic group over a finite field is trivial.
At the two witness primes actually used the bound is not even close: `ℓ = 3`
gives `≥ 4 − 2√3 ≈ 0.54`, `ℓ = 5` gives `≥ 6 − 2√5 ≈ 1.53`.

**`hN` IS LOAD-BEARING, and not merely for the intended content**: the
Weil bound is `q + 1 − 2g√q`, which is NEGATIVE as soon as `g` is large
relative to `q`, and pointless smooth proper geometrically connected curves
over small finite fields genuinely exist in higher genus.  So the argument
— and, for all this file knows, the statement — fails without genus `1`,
and `hN` is the only thing that supplies it.

**`hℓN` IS LOAD-BEARING FOR THE ARGUMENT** in the same indirect way: it is
what makes `x0Genus N` the genus of THIS special fibre rather than of the
generic one, i.e. what makes the reduction good.  It is exactly the
hypothesis `exists_x0Compactification_finiteField` needs to produce the
compactification at all, so the consumer already holds it and passing it in
costs nothing.

