namespace FixedLocusOfAdditive

variable {N : ℕ} (d : Gamma0Datum N (Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))))
  (v : d.E ⟶ d.E) (hv : v ≫ d.f = d.f)

section CMPhiYoneda

open _root_.CategoryTheory _root_.AlgebraicGeometry _root_.Fermat in
/-- **LEAF 1a (cut 2026-07-31): the ALGEBRAISATION — a Weierstrass model turns an
endomorphism of `E(ℚ̄)` carrying `IsIsogeny` into a scheme endomorphism of the
datum.**  This is all the geometry of LEAF 1, and it is now all that is left of
it: the arithmetic half is PROVEN below (see the `hp` paragraph).

**LEVEL-GENERIC.**  `N` is unconstrained, there is no `hp`, and — this is the
point of the seam — **the level structure does not appear in the statement at
all**.  Only `d₀.ab`, `d.ab` and the Weierstrass model are consumed.  So this
leaf is equally the geometry needed by any other CM-descent in this tree; a
prover should not specialise it back to `p ∈ {43, 67, 163}`.

**WHAT IT PRODUCES, and why there are TWO point identifications.**

* `Ψ`, the scheme endomorphism of `d.E` over `Spec ℚ̄`, with `IsAdditiveOn`;
* `ε`, an additive identification of `E(ℚ̄)` with the `ℚ̄`-points of `d`
  (`RelPoint d.f (𝟙 …)`).  This is the group in which the FIXED-LOCUS clause of
  LEAF 1 is stated, and `hint` — `post Ψ ∘ ε = ε ∘ φ` — is what turns that
  clause into a statement about `ker (2φ − 1)`;
* `ε₀`, the same identification read as GEOMETRIC POINTS OF THE `ℚ`-DATUM
  (`GeomFibrePt d₀.f (𝟙 SpecQ)`), where the absolute Galois group acts.  It is a
  separate conjunct because `RelPoint d.f (𝟙 (Spec ℚ̄))` carries **no** Galois
  action: precomposition with `specGal σ` moves the base point off `𝟙`, so
  Galois-equivariance is not even statable on `ε`'s target.  Over `Spec ℚ` it is,
  and that is where `d₀.cyc`'s stability lives.

  The two are tied by `hlink`, `(ε₀ x).1 = (ε x).1 ≫ bc.map` — one equation of
  morphisms, and exactly what `IsBaseChangeOf.liesIn_iff` needs to move
  `RelPoint.LiesIn` between the two sides.  A prover gets `hlink` for free: both
  identifications come from the SAME isomorphism of schemes, `ε₀` being `ε`
  followed by the base-change map.

* the minimal polynomial at the TAUTOLOGICAL point.  It is asked for here rather
  than derived downstream because it is an equation between two morphisms
  `d.E ⟶ d.E`, not a statement about `ℚ̄`-points: recovering it from `hint`
  alone would need "a morphism out of `d.E` is determined by its `ℚ̄`-points",
  which is a further reducedness/density argument.  For whoever proves this leaf
  it is free, since `Ψ` is transported from `End(E⁄ℚ̄)` along an ISOMORPHISM and
  the transport is a ring map, so `hsq` transports verbatim.

**THE ROUTE, and the first three steps are already PROVEN.**

1. `hmodel` gives an open immersion `ι : weierstrassAffine E ⟶ d₀.E` over
   `Spec ℚ` whose range is the complement of the zero section, and
   `exists_affineChart_projModel` gives the matching chart of
   `WeierstrassCurve.Projective.proj E`; `Fermat.exists_isIso_of_affineChart`
   (`ModularCurve/EllipticScheme.lean`) turns the pair into an ISOMORPHISM
   `proj E ≅ d₀.E` over `Spec ℚ`, pinned by `ι₀ ≫ u = ι`.  **NAMEABILITY IS AN
   OBSTACLE HERE AND IT IS NOT A THEOREM**: `X0.lean` reaches
   `ModularCurve/EllipticScheme` through a NON-public import, so that name
   cannot be cited from this file.  What is needed is a re-export in `X0.lean`
   written in `Gamma0Datum`/`IsWeierstrassModel` vocabulary, exactly like the
   existing `exists_weierstrassModel_geomFibreAddEquiv_of_gamma0Datum` — a
   mechanical addition.  (`exists_isIso_of_affineChart` is itself still
   `sorryAx`-tainted, through `projGroupLaw`, which occurs in its own
   HYPOTHESES; that taint is somebody else's leaf and does not block writing
   this proof.)
2. `φ` carries `IsIsogeny`, hence `IsRationalMap`: polynomials `A B C D E` over
   `ℚ̄` computing the coordinates of `φ P` away from `Z(B) ∪ Z(E)`.  That is a
   morphism out of a dense open of the affine chart, and
   `AlgebraicGeometry.exists_unique_extension_of_isSmoothProperCurve`
   (`Mathlib/AlgebraicGeometry/CurveExtension.lean`, PROVEN, no sorry) extends
   it across that finite bad locus into the PROPER target.  **Turning the
   `IsRationalMap` certificate into a morphism of schemes out of that dense open
   is the natural first sub-leaf** if this one is cut further.
3. `bc` transports from `Spec ℚ` to `Spec ℚ̄`.  Note this wants `proj` to commute
   with base change; `grep` for it before relying on it, and if it is absent the
   cheaper route is to transport along `bc.isPullback` directly, since the
   conclusion never mentions `proj`.
4. additivity is `Fermat.isAdditiveOn_of_post_zero` (rigidity: a morphism of
   abelian schemes fixing the zero section is a homomorphism).

**ALGEBRAICITY IS IN THE STATEMENT AND MUST STAY THERE.**  `φ` is a
`WeierstrassCurve.End`, which carries `IsIsogeny` and hence `IsRationalMap`; the
leaf is FALSE with `φ` weakened to an additive endomorphism of the point group.
As an abstract group `E(ℚ̄) ≅ (ℚ/ℤ)² ⊕ ⊕_𝔠 ℚ`, so its free part is a
`ℚ`-vector space of continuum dimension and carries `ℚ`-linear endomorphisms
with minimal polynomial `X² − X + n` for any `n` — nowhere near algebraic, and
no such map is `post Ψ` for a morphism of schemes.

**`hmodel` IS LOAD-BEARING** for the same reason: it is the only hypothesis
tying the SCHEME `d₀.E` to the coordinates in which `φ`'s certificate is
written.  Without it `d₀` is an arbitrary elliptic scheme and `φ` an
endomorphism of an unrelated curve.

**NOT VACUOUS**: `hmodel` is satisfiable for every `d₀` — that is
`Fermat.exists_weierstrassModel_geomFibreAddEquiv_of_gamma0Datum`, PROVEN — and
`φ` with `hsq` is satisfiable by `exists_endMinpoly_of_stable_cyclic_mazurLevel`
above at `n = (p+1)/4`, `p ∈ {43, 67, 163}`; `n = 0` with `φ = 0` realises it at
every level. -/
theorem exists_relSchemeEnd_geomEquiv_of_weierstrassModel {N : ℕ} (n : ℕ)
    {d₀ : Gamma0Datum N SpecQ}
    {d : Gamma0Datum N (Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)))}
    (bc : IsBaseChangeOf (specAlgClos ℚ) d d₀)
    (E : WeierstrassCurve ℚ) [E.IsElliptic]
    (hmodel : IsWeierstrassModel d₀.ab E)
    (φ : WeierstrassCurve.End (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).toAffine)
    (hsq : φ * φ + (n : WeierstrassCurve.End (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).toAffine) = φ) :
    ∃ (Ψ : d.E ⟶ d.E) (hΨ : Ψ ≫ d.f = d.f),
      IsAdditiveOn d.ab d.ab Ψ hΨ ∧
      (letI := d.ab.addCommGroup (𝟙 (Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))))
       letI := d₀.ab.addCommGroup (specAlgClos ℚ ≫ 𝟙 SpecQ)
       ∃ (ε : (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).Point ≃+
             RelPoint d.f (𝟙 (Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)))))
         (ε₀ : (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).Point ≃+ GeomFibrePt d₀.f (𝟙 SpecQ)),
         (∀ x : (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).Point, (ε₀ x).1 = (ε x).1 ≫ bc.map) ∧
         (∀ (σ : Field.absoluteGaloisGroup ℚ) (x : (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).Point),
            ε₀ (WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.map
                (σ : AlgebraicClosure ℚ ≃ₐ[ℚ] AlgebraicClosure ℚ).toAlgHom x)
              = d₀.ab.galSMul (𝟙 SpecQ) σ (ε₀ x)) ∧
         (∀ x : (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).Point,
            RelPoint.post Ψ hΨ (ε x)
              = ε ((φ : AddMonoid.End (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).toAffine.Point) x))) ∧
      (letI := d.ab.addCommGroup d.f;
        d.ab.add (RelPoint.post Ψ hΨ (RelPoint.post Ψ hΨ (RelPoint.self d.f)))
            (n • RelPoint.self d.f) = RelPoint.post Ψ hΨ (RelPoint.self d.f)) :=
  sorry

/-- **THE CM ENDOMORPHISM IS POSTCOMPOSITION WITH A MORPHISM OF SCHEMES**
(PROVEN 2026-07-31, flt-lean-393 — Yoneda, and nothing else).

`h.phi` is defined at every test scheme; `phi_pre` makes it natural.  Evaluating at
the universal relative point `⟨𝟙 d.E, _⟩` gives `Φ := (h.phi ⟨𝟙 d.E, _⟩).1`, a
morphism `d.E ⟶ d.E` over the base, and naturality read at `h := x.1` says
`h.phi x = x.1 ≫ Φ` for every relative point `x` at every base point.

This is the step that makes the transport in
`exists_end_of_isWeierstrassModel_of_isAdditiveOn` attackable at all: an
`IsIsogeny` certificate is polynomials in the coordinates, and those can only be
read off a MORPHISM.  Nothing here is specific to `p` or to complex
multiplication — only `phi` and `phi_pre` are used. -/
theorem exists_morphism_phi {p : ℕ} {T : Scheme.{0}} {d : Gamma0Datum p T}
    (h : IsCMByRamifiedMaximalOrder p d) :
    ∃ (Φ : d.E ⟶ d.E) (hΦ : Φ ≫ d.f = d.f),
      ∀ {T' : Scheme.{0}} {g : T' ⟶ T} (x : RelPoint d.f g),
        h.phi x = RelPoint.post Φ hΦ x := by
  refine ⟨(h.phi (⟨𝟙 d.E, Category.id_comp d.f⟩ : RelPoint d.f d.f)).1,
    (h.phi (⟨𝟙 d.E, Category.id_comp d.f⟩ : RelPoint d.f d.f)).2, ?_⟩
  intro T' g x
  have hx : RelPoint.pre x.1 x.2 (⟨𝟙 d.E, Category.id_comp d.f⟩ : RelPoint d.f d.f) = x :=
    Subtype.ext (Category.comp_id x.1)
  have hp := h.phi_pre x.1 x.2 (⟨𝟙 d.E, Category.id_comp d.f⟩ : RelPoint d.f d.f)
  rw [hx] at hp
  exact hp

/-- **THE WEIERSTRASS BRIDGE** (sorry leaf, cut 2026-07-31 by flt-lean-393 out of
`exists_end_of_isCMByRamifiedMaximalOrder`; it replaces that leaf and is the only
one the cut leaves open): a Weierstrass model of an elliptic scheme over `ℚ̄`
identifies the `ℚ̄`-points of the model with the relative points of the scheme over
the identity, and carries an additive endomorphism of the latter into
`WeierstrassCurve.End`.

TRUE, and it contains NO complex multiplication and no arithmetic: `p`, the CM
relation and `IsCMByRamifiedMaximalOrder` have all been removed by the cut, and
what remains holds for every elliptic scheme over `ℚ̄`, every Weierstrass model of
it and every additive `Φ`.

**THE TWO HALVES, and why they may NOT be separated into two leaves.**
The equivalence `e` is the geometric identification supplied by the chart of
`IsWeierstrassModel`; `ψ` is `Φ` read through it.  Stating them as two leaves — "an
additive equivalence exists" and "any additive equivalence transports `Φ` into
`End W`" — makes the SECOND one FALSE: composing a geometric `e` with a wild
automorphism of the abstract group `W(ℚ̄)` (divisible of infinite rank, so it has
many) again gives a `≃+`, and the transported endomorphism is then a group
endomorphism with no reason to satisfy `IsRationalMap`.  The `≃+` type does not
record that `e` came from the chart, so the two must be produced together.  See the
subsection note above.

**THE ARGUMENT.**  `hW` gives an open immersion `Spec ℚ̄[W] ↪ d.E` over `Spec ℚ̄`
whose range is the complement of the zero section, so the `ℚ̄`-points of `W` off the
origin match the relative points of `d.f` off the origin, and the one missing point
on each side is the origin.  Rigidity (`isAdditiveOn_of_post_zero`, over an
arbitrary base) then makes the resulting bijection additive: the chord–tangent law
and `d.ab`'s functor-of-points law are two group laws on the same genus-one curve
with the same identity.  `Fermat.exists_geomFibreAddEquiv_of_weierstrassModel`
(`ModularCurve/EllipticScheme.lean`) is exactly this over `ℚ` and is PROVEN; it is
NOT importable here (`X0.lean` imports that file privately), so its pieces must be
rebuilt.  The chart-extension half is the cheap one — the valuative criterion
`AlgebraicGeometry.exists_unique_extension_of_isSmoothProperCurve` is reachable and
stated over an arbitrary field — and whatever proves
`nonempty_isEllipticIsoOf_of_variableChange_isWeierstrassModel` below will already
have restated the small ℚ-only wrappers over `ℚ̄`.

**WHAT IS GENUINELY NEW BEYOND THE `ℚ` MODEL: the `IsIsogeny` certificate.**
`exists_geomFibreAddEquiv_of_weierstrassModel` concludes only a `≃+`, and
membership in `WeierstrassCurve.End` is strictly more: `IsRationalMap` (polynomials
`A, B, C, D, E` with `x(ψP) · B(xP) = A(xP)` and
`y(ψP) · E(xP) = C(xP) · y(P) + D(xP)`), surjectivity of a nonzero `ψ`, and
finiteness of its kernel.  All three come from `Φ` being a MORPHISM: restricted to
the affine chart it is a map of coordinate rings `ℚ̄[W] → ℚ̄[W]`, and
`ℚ̄[W] = ℚ̄[x, y]/(W)` is free of rank two over `ℚ̄[x]`, so the images of `x` and `y`
are exactly of the certificate's shape; surjectivity and finite kernel are then
properness of `Φ` and dimension.  `exists_morphism_phi` above is what supplies the
morphism, and it is the reason this leaf is stated with `Φ` rather than with a
functorial family.

**`hadd` IS LOAD-BEARING and the leaf is FALSE without it.**  A morphism over the
base need not respect the group law — translation by a nonzero `ℚ̄`-point is one —
and then no `ψ ∈ End W` can intertwine, `End W` consisting of ADDITIVE maps.

NOT VACUOUS: `exists_isWeierstrassModel_algClos` supplies `W` and `hW`, and
`exists_morphism_phi` together with `phi_add` supplies `Φ` and `hadd` at each of
`p = 43, 67, 163` — see the `IsCMByRamifiedMaximalOrder` docstring for the
`ellisomat` check that the hypotheses are satisfiable there. -/
theorem exists_end_of_isWeierstrassModel_of_isAdditiveOn {N : ℕ}
    {d : Gamma0Datum N (Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)))}
    (W : _root_.WeierstrassCurve (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)) [W.IsElliptic]
    (hW : IsWeierstrassModel d.ab W)
    (Φ : d.E ⟶ d.E) (hΦ : Φ ≫ d.f = d.f) (hadd : IsAdditiveOn d.ab d.ab Φ hΦ) :
    letI := d.ab.addCommGroup (𝟙 (Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))))
    ∃ (ψ : _root_.WeierstrassCurve.End W.toAffine)
      (e : W.toAffine.Point ≃+
        RelPoint d.f (𝟙 (Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))))),
      ∀ P : W.toAffine.Point,
        e ((ψ : AddMonoid.End W.toAffine.Point) P) = RelPoint.post Φ hΦ (e P) :=
  sorry

end CMPhiYoneda

/-- `x : ℚ̄` is a **CM `j`-invariant for the order `ℤ[X]/(X² − X + m)`**, of
discriminant `1 − 4m`: it is the `j`-invariant of an elliptic curve over `ℚ̄` whose
FULL geometric endomorphism ring is generated by a single `φ` with `φ² + m = φ`.

This is `MazurCMForm.IsCMJInvariant` with the relation `ψ² = [−n]` — which pins the
order `ℤ[√−n]` of discriminant `−4n`, never fundamental — replaced by the relation
`φ² − φ + m = 0`, whose discriminant `1 − 4m` is ODD and therefore CAN be
fundamental.  At `m = (p+1)/4` it is `−p`.  See the subsection note above for why
the two predicates are genuinely different and must not be identified.

The `hrel`/`htop` pair is faithful exactly when `1 − 4m < 0`: otherwise `X² − X + m`
may have an integer root, `φ = [c]` is allowed, `htop` says only `End = ℤ`, and the
predicate holds at every non-CM `j`-invariant.  Both leaves below therefore carry
that negativity, in the form `0 < 4m − 1`; see their FALSITY AUDITs. -/
def IsCMJInvariantOfRel (m : ℤ) (x : AlgebraicClosure ℚ) : Prop :=
  ∃ (W : _root_.WeierstrassCurve (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)) (hW : W.IsElliptic),
    letI := hW
    W.j = x ∧ ∃ φ : _root_.WeierstrassCurve.End W.toAffine,
      φ * φ + ((m : ℤ) : _root_.WeierstrassCurve.End W.toAffine) = φ ∧
      Subring.closure ({φ} : Set (_root_.WeierstrassCurve.End W.toAffine)) = ⊤

/-- **LEAF (cut 2026-07-31, flt-lean-393) — THE CLASS POLYNOMIAL OF THE ORDER
`ℤ[X]/(X² − X + m)` IS IRREDUCIBLE OVER `ℚ`**, stated as: any two CM `j`-invariants
of that order have the SAME minimal polynomial over `ℚ`.

This is the first main theorem of complex multiplication and NOTHING ELSE: it is
uniform in `m`, mentions no prime, no level and no class number, and it is the only
statement in the cut of `jInvariant_eq_of_end_closure_eq_top` that needs the ring
class field.

**THE ARGUMENT** (Cox, *Primes of the Form x² + ny²*, §11.1, Theorem 11.1;
Silverman *ATAEC* II.4.3 and II.6.1).  With `D = 1 − 4m < 0`, `K = ℚ(√D)` and
`O = ℤ[(1 + √D)/2]` the order of discriminant `D`, the `h(O)` curves with CM by `O`
have `j`-invariants forming a single `Gal(ℚ̄/K)`-orbit, through the Artin
isomorphism `Cl(O) ≅ Gal(K(j(𝔞))/K)` of the ring class field; equivalently the class
polynomial `H_D(X) = ∏_{[𝔞] ∈ Cl(O)} (X − j(𝔞))` — which has RATIONAL coefficients,
being stable under complex conjugation as well — is irreducible over `ℚ`.  Its roots
are exactly the CM `j`-invariants of `O`, so any two of them share it as their
minimal polynomial.

**FALSITY AUDIT — `hm : 0 < 4 * m - 1` IS LOAD-BEARING, with an explicit
counterexample.**  Take `m = 0`.  The relation reads `φ * φ + 0 = φ`, which the
IDENTITY `φ = 1` satisfies, and `Subring.closure {(1 : End W)}` is the image of `ℤ`,
which is `⊤` for every curve with `End(W) = ℤ` — i.e. for every NON-CM curve over
`ℚ̄`.  So `IsCMJInvariantOfRel 0` holds at `j = 5` and at `j = 2^(1/3)`, whose
minimal polynomials `X − 5` and `X³ − 2` are different, and the conclusion fails.
The same collapse happens at `m = −2` (`φ = [2]`: `4 − 2 = 2`).  With
`0 < 4m − 1` the polynomial `X² − X + m` has negative discriminant, so `φ` is not an
integer multiplication and `End(W)` really is the imaginary quadratic order.

**NOT VACUOUS**: satisfiable at `m = 1` (`D = −3`, the curve `y² = x³ + 1` with `φ`
a primitive sixth root of unity) and at `m = 11, 17, 41` (`D = −43, −67, −163`, the
CM curves of the tabulated `j`-invariants).  At `m = 6`, `D = −23`, `h(−23) = 3` and
`polclass(-23) = x³ + 3491750x² − 5151296875x + 12771880859375` is irreducible over
`ℚ` (PARI/GP, 2026-07-31), so the conclusion there is a genuine cubic coincidence
and a wrong proof can fail.

**THE CHECK THAT WOULD REFUTE IT**: two elliptic curves over `ℚ̄`, each with
endomorphism ring generated by a root of `X² − X + m` for the same `m` with
`4m > 1`, whose `j`-invariants have different minimal polynomials over `ℚ`. -/
theorem minpoly_eq_of_isCMJInvariantOfRel (m : ℤ) (hm : 0 < 4 * m - 1)
    (x y : AlgebraicClosure ℚ)
    (hx : IsCMJInvariantOfRel m x) (hy : IsCMJInvariantOfRel m y) :
    minpoly ℚ y = minpoly ℚ x :=
  sorry

/-- **LEAF (cut 2026-07-31, flt-lean-393) — CLASS NUMBER ONE AT `43, 67, 163`, in
the form `[ℚ(j) : ℚ] = 1`**: a CM `j`-invariant for the maximal order of
discriminant `−p` is RATIONAL.

This is the ONLY place `p ∈ {43, 67, 163}` enters the cut of
`jInvariant_eq_of_end_closure_eq_top`; the companion leaf
`minpoly_eq_of_isCMJInvariantOfRel` is uniform in the order.

**THE ARGUMENT.**  `hx` says `End(W) = ℤ[φ]` with `φ² − φ + (p+1)/4 = 0`, the order
of discriminant `1 − (p + 1) = −p`, which at a prime `p ≡ 3 mod 4` is FUNDAMENTAL,
so it is the maximal order `O_{−p}` of `ℚ(√−p)`.  The first main theorem gives
`[ℚ(j(W)) : ℚ] = h(−p)`, and `h(−43) = h(−67) = h(−163) = 1` (PARI/GP `qfbclassno`,
re-verified 2026-07-31).  Hence `j(W) ∈ ℚ`; the three values are `−884736000`,
`−147197952000`, `−262537412640768000` (`polclass(-p)`, linear at all three levels,
same run).  A successor may of course prove the literal and read rationality off it
— the literal is the stronger statement, and it is not asked for here only because
it drags in the Hilbert class polynomial as a definition.

**FALSITY AUDIT — `hp` IS LOAD-BEARING TWICE, with an explicit counterexample.**
First, `(p + 1) / 4` is NATURAL division and is exact only when `p ≡ 3 mod 4`;
at other `p` the discriminant is not `−p` and the statement is about a different
order.  Second, and independently, at `p = 23` — prime, `≡ 3 mod 4`, so
`(23 + 1)/4 = 6` is exact and the order IS the maximal order of discriminant `−23`
— the conclusion is FALSE: `h(−23) = 3` and `polclass(-23)` is the irreducible cubic
`x³ + 3491750x² − 5151296875x + 12771880859375`, so every CM `j`-invariant of
`O_{−23}` has degree `3` over `ℚ` and none of them is rational (PARI/GP, 2026-07-31,
including the irreducibility check).

**NOT VACUOUS**: at each of the three levels the hypothesis is satisfied by the CM
curve of the tabulated `j`-invariant with its ramified `p`-isogeny (see the
`IsCMByRamifiedMaximalOrder` docstring for the `ellisomat` check), and the two
declarations above supply `hrel` and `htop` for it. -/
theorem exists_rat_of_isCMJInvariantOfRel (p : ℕ)
    (hp : p ∈ ({43, 67, 163} : Finset ℕ)) (x : AlgebraicClosure ℚ)
    (hx : IsCMJInvariantOfRel (((p + 1) / 4 : ℕ) : ℤ) x) :
    ∃ q : ℚ, x = algebraMap ℚ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) q :=
  sorry

/-- The tautological equation of the equaliser, with the `𝟙` evaluated away. -/
theorem iota_comp_self : equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ v = equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) := by
  simpa using equalizer.condition v (𝟙 d.E)

/-- The relative point of `d.E` underlying an `R`-point of the fixed locus. -/
def fixPt (R : CommAlgCat.{0} (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))
    (w : SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R) :
    RelPoint d.f
      (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) R))) :=
  ⟨w.1 ≫ equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E), by rw [Category.assoc]; exact w.2⟩

theorem fixPt_fixed (R : CommAlgCat.{0} (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))
    (w : SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R) :
    (fixPt d v R w).1 ≫ v = (fixPt d v R w).1 := by
  show (w.1 ≫ equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E)) ≫ v = w.1 ≫ equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E)
  rw [Category.assoc, iota_comp_self]

theorem fixPt_injective (R : CommAlgCat.{0} (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)) :
    Function.Injective (fixPt d v R) := by
  intro a b hab
  refine Subtype.ext ?_
  have h : a.1 ≫ equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) = b.1 ≫ equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) :=
    congrArg Subtype.val hab
  exact (cancel_mono (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E))).1 h

/-- Conversely a relative point fixed by `v` is an `R`-point of the fixed locus. -/
def fixLift (R : CommAlgCat.{0} (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))
    (x : RelPoint d.f
      (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) R))))
    (hx : x.1 ≫ v = x.1) :
    SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R :=
  ⟨equalizer.lift x.1 (by rw [hx, Category.comp_id]), by
    rw [← Category.assoc, equalizer.lift_ι]; exact x.2⟩

theorem fixPt_fixLift (R : CommAlgCat.{0} (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))
    (x : RelPoint d.f
      (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) R))))
    (hx : x.1 ≫ v = x.1) : fixPt d v R (fixLift d v R x hx) = x :=
  Subtype.ext (equalizer.lift_ι _ _)

variable (hadd : IsAdditiveOn d.ab d.ab v hv)

/-- Being fixed by `v` is the same as being fixed by `RelPoint.post v hv`. -/
theorem post_eq_iff {T : Scheme.{0}}
    {g : T ⟶ Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))} (x : RelPoint d.f g) :
    RelPoint.post v hv x = x ↔ x.1 ≫ v = x.1 :=
  ⟨fun h => congrArg Subtype.val h, fun h => Subtype.ext h⟩

include hv hadd in
theorem add_fixed {T : Scheme.{0}}
    {g : T ⟶ Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))} {x y : RelPoint d.f g}
    (hx : x.1 ≫ v = x.1) (hy : y.1 ≫ v = y.1) :
    (d.ab.add x y).1 ≫ v = (d.ab.add x y).1 := by
  refine (post_eq_iff d v hv _).1 ?_
  rw [hadd x y, (post_eq_iff d v hv x).2 hx, (post_eq_iff d v hv y).2 hy]

include hv hadd in
theorem zero_fixed {T : Scheme.{0}}
    (g : T ⟶ Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))) :
    (d.ab.zero g).1 ≫ v = (d.ab.zero g).1 :=
  (post_eq_iff d v hv _).1 (hadd.post_zero g)

include hv hadd in
theorem neg_fixed {T : Scheme.{0}}
    {g : T ⟶ Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))} {x : RelPoint d.f g}
    (hx : x.1 ≫ v = x.1) : (d.ab.neg x).1 ≫ v = (d.ab.neg x).1 := by
  refine (post_eq_iff d v hv _).1 ?_
  rw [hadd.post_neg x, (post_eq_iff d v hv x).2 hx]

include hv hadd in
/-- The group law on the `R`-points of the fixed locus. -/
def ptsMul (R : CommAlgCat.{0} (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))
    (a b : SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R) :
    SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R :=
  fixLift d v R (d.ab.add (fixPt d v R a) (fixPt d v R b))
    (add_fixed d v hv hadd (fixPt_fixed d v R a) (fixPt_fixed d v R b))

include hv hadd in
def ptsOne (R : CommAlgCat.{0} (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)) :
    SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R :=
  fixLift d v R (d.ab.zero _) (zero_fixed d v hv hadd _)

include hv hadd in
def ptsInv (R : CommAlgCat.{0} (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))
    (a : SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R) :
    SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R :=
  fixLift d v R (d.ab.neg (fixPt d v R a)) (neg_fixed d v hv hadd (fixPt_fixed d v R a))

theorem fixPt_ptsMul (R : CommAlgCat.{0} (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))
    (a b : SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R) :
    fixPt d v R (ptsMul d v hv hadd R a b)
      = d.ab.add (fixPt d v R a) (fixPt d v R b) :=
  fixPt_fixLift _ _ _ _ _

theorem fixPt_ptsOne (R : CommAlgCat.{0} (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)) :
    fixPt d v R (ptsOne d v hv hadd R) = d.ab.zero _ :=
  fixPt_fixLift _ _ _ _ _

theorem fixPt_ptsInv (R : CommAlgCat.{0} (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))
    (a : SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R) :
    fixPt d v R (ptsInv d v hv hadd R a) = d.ab.neg (fixPt d v R a) :=
  fixPt_fixLift _ _ _ _ _

/-- The group of `R`-points of the fixed locus, written multiplicatively for `GrpCat`. -/
@[reducible] def ptsGroup (R : CommAlgCat.{0} (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)) :
    Group (SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R) :=
  letI : Mul (SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R) := ⟨ptsMul d v hv hadd R⟩
  letI : One (SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R) := ⟨ptsOne d v hv hadd R⟩
  letI : Inv (SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R) := ⟨ptsInv d v hv hadd R⟩
  Group.ofLeftAxioms
    (fun a b c => fixPt_injective d v R (by
      show fixPt d v R (ptsMul d v hv hadd R (ptsMul d v hv hadd R a b) c)
        = fixPt d v R (ptsMul d v hv hadd R a (ptsMul d v hv hadd R b c))
      rw [fixPt_ptsMul, fixPt_ptsMul, fixPt_ptsMul, fixPt_ptsMul, d.ab.add_assoc]))
    (fun a => fixPt_injective d v R (by
      show fixPt d v R (ptsMul d v hv hadd R (ptsOne d v hv hadd R) a) = fixPt d v R a
      rw [fixPt_ptsMul, fixPt_ptsOne, d.ab.zero_add]))
    (fun a => fixPt_injective d v R (by
      show fixPt d v R (ptsMul d v hv hadd R (ptsInv d v hv hadd R a) a)
        = fixPt d v R (ptsOne d v hv hadd R)
      rw [fixPt_ptsMul, fixPt_ptsInv, fixPt_ptsOne, d.ab.neg_add]))

/-- Base change of `R`-points along a `K`-algebra map. -/
def ptsPre {R S : CommAlgCat.{0} (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)} (ψ : R ⟶ S)
    (a : SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R) :
    SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) S :=
  ⟨Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom ψ.hom.toRingHom) ≫ a.1, by
    rw [Category.assoc, a.2, CyclicSubgroupOfOrder.specAlg_comp]⟩

theorem fixPt_ptsPre {R S : CommAlgCat.{0} (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)} (ψ : R ⟶ S)
    (a : SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R) :
    fixPt d v S (ptsPre d v ψ a)
      = RelPoint.pre (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom ψ.hom.toRingHom))
          (CyclicSubgroupOfOrder.specAlg_comp ψ) (fixPt d v R a) :=
  Subtype.ext (Category.assoc _ _ _)

theorem ptsPre_ptsMul {R S : CommAlgCat.{0} (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)} (ψ : R ⟶ S)
    (a b : SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R) :
    ptsPre d v ψ (ptsMul d v hv hadd R a b)
      = ptsMul d v hv hadd S (ptsPre d v ψ a) (ptsPre d v ψ b) :=
  fixPt_injective d v S (by
    rw [fixPt_ptsPre, fixPt_ptsMul, fixPt_ptsMul, fixPt_ptsPre, fixPt_ptsPre, d.ab.pre_add])

/-- **The functor of points of the fixed locus**, valued in groups.  This is the
one input `Fermat.exists_hopfAlgebra_of_ptsFunctor` asks for.

The `Group` instances are hoisted into a single `letI grp : ∀ R, …` ahead of the
structure literal rather than introduced inside `obj` and `map` separately, for
the reason `X0.lean`'s `kerPtsFunctor` does the same: `map` has to typecheck as a
morphism `obj R ⟶ obj S`, which needs the instance in `map` to be the SAME TERM as
the one `obj` used, and two independent `letI`s are not seen as such. -/
def ptsFunctor : CommAlgCat.{0} (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) ⥤ GrpCat.{0} :=
  letI grp : ∀ R : CommAlgCat.{0} (AlgebraicClosure ℚ),
      Group (SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R) := ptsGroup d v hv hadd
  { obj := fun R => GrpCat.of (SchemePts (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) R)
    map := fun {R S} ψ =>
      GrpCat.ofHom (MonoidHom.mk' (ptsPre d v ψ) (ptsPre_ptsMul d v hv hadd ψ))
    map_id := fun R => by
      apply GrpCat.hom_ext
      refine MonoidHom.ext fun a => Subtype.ext ?_
      show Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (CommAlgCat.Hom.hom (𝟙 R)).toRingHom) ≫ a.1 = a.1
      rw [show CommRingCat.ofHom (CommAlgCat.Hom.hom (𝟙 R)).toRingHom
          = 𝟙 (CommRingCat.of (R : Type)) from rfl,
        Spec.map_id, Category.id_comp]
    map_comp := fun {R S U} ψ ρ => by
      apply GrpCat.hom_ext
      refine MonoidHom.ext fun a => Subtype.ext ?_
      show Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (CommAlgCat.Hom.hom (ψ ≫ ρ)).toRingHom) ≫ a.1
        = Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom ρ.hom.toRingHom) ≫
          Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom ψ.hom.toRingHom) ≫ a.1
      rw [← Category.assoc, ← Spec.map_comp]
      rfl }

end FixedLocusOfAdditive

/-- **The fixed locus of an additive `v` is `Spec` of a finite commutative Hopf
algebra over `ℚ̄`** (PROVEN 2026-07-31) — `Fermat.exists_hopfAlgebra_of_ptsFunctor`
applied to `FixedLocusOfAdditive.ptsFunctor`.

The two side conditions of that theorem are both trivial here because the
bijection `β` is the IDENTITY: the `R`-points of `Fix(v)` are literally the
carrier of the group, so `β = Equiv.refl` and the base-change compatibility `hβ`
is `Subtype.ext` on the one component. -/
theorem exists_hopfAlgebra_fixedLocus {N : ℕ}
    (d : Gamma0Datum N (Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))))
    (v : d.E ⟶ d.E) (hv : v ≫ d.f = d.f) (hadd : IsAdditiveOn d.ab d.ab v hv)
    [IsFinite (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f)] :
    ∃ (A : Type) (_ : CommRing A) (_ : _root_.HopfAlgebra (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) A)
      (_ : Module.Finite (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) A),
      Nonempty (equalizer v (𝟙 d.E) ≅ Spec (CommRingCat.of A)) :=
  exists_hopfAlgebra_of_ptsFunctor (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f)
    (FixedLocusOfAdditive.ptsFunctor d v hv hadd) (fun _ => Equiv.refl _)
    (fun _ _ _ _ _ hb => Subtype.ext hb)

/-- **CARTIER FOR THE FIXED LOCUS: if `Fix(v)` is finite over `Spec ℚ̄` and `v` is
additive, then `Fix(v)` is REDUCED** (PROVEN 2026-07-31) — the reducedness half of
`isFinite_isReduced_fixedLocus_of_geomFixed` below, discharged exactly along the
route that leaf's docstring prescribes ("*ADD an additivity hypothesis on `v` …
that trade is a strict improvement, since it replaces a missing theory by a
theorem already on disk*").

**Characteristic zero is the whole content and it is spent in one place**, the
appeal to `CartierTheorem.isReduced_of_charZero`.  In residue characteristic `p`
the statement is false, with the same witness as `isReduced_kerHom`'s docstring in
`X0.lean` gives: `ker F ⊆ 𝔾_a` over `𝔽̄_p` is finite flat of rank `p` and
non-reduced.  That is why the base of a `Gamma0Datum` here is spelled
`Spec (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)`.

**`hgeom` is NOT needed for this half.**  The leaf below consumes `hgeom` twice —
once to exclude `v = 𝟙` (where `Fix(𝟙) = d.E` is not finite) and once for the
containment — but both of those uses are about FINITENESS.  Given finiteness, the
group-scheme argument needs nothing beyond `hv` and `hadd`; in particular this
theorem is not vacuous at `v = 𝟙`, it is simply unusable there, since its
`IsFinite` instance argument is then unavailable. -/
theorem isReduced_fixedLocus_of_isAdditiveOn {N : ℕ}
    (d : Gamma0Datum N (Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))))
    (v : d.E ⟶ d.E) (hv : v ≫ d.f = d.f) (hadd : IsAdditiveOn d.ab d.ab v hv)
    [IsFinite (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f)] :
    AlgebraicGeometry.IsReduced (equalizer v (𝟙 d.E)) := by
  obtain ⟨A, _, _, _, ⟨e⟩⟩ := exists_hopfAlgebra_fixedLocus d v hv hadd
  haveI : _root_.IsReduced A :=
    _root_.CartierTheorem.isReduced_of_charZero (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) A
  exact AlgebraicGeometry.isReduced_of_isOpenImmersion e.hom

end

end FixedLocusHopf

