statement; the paragraphs that follow are the ones that describe THIS content and
they are the reason the split is worth making.  There is no scheme-theoretic
bookkeeping left in it: no `RelPoint`, no level structure in the conclusion, one
explicit scheme and two properties of it.

**HOW `hgeom` ENTERS, and it is not decoration** — it is what excludes `v = 𝟙`,
where the statement is FALSE (`Fix(𝟙) = d.E`, which is proper of relative
dimension `1` over `Spec ℚ̄` and so not finite).  See the degenerate-case
paragraph below for how it excludes it: `hgeom` at `v = 𝟙` says every `ℚ̄`-point
of `d.E` lies in `C`, while `d.cyc.geom_cyclic` pins the `ℚ̄`-points of `C` to a
cyclic group of order `N`, and `d.E` has infinitely many.  A prover must run that
step; it cannot be skipped, and it is the one place the hypothesis is consumed
for finiteness rather than for reducedness.

**FALSITY AUDIT (2026-07-30, run against THIS statement and not inherited).**
The statement is TRUE.  For finiteness: `hgeom` gives `v ≠ 𝟙`; a morphism `v` of
the elliptic curve `d.E` over its base is `τ_Q ∘ ψ`, so `Fix(v)` is the fibre of
`ψ − 1` over `−Q`, which is finite unless `ψ = 1 ∧ Q = 0`, i.e. unless `v = 𝟙`.
(When `ψ = 1` and `Q ≠ 0`, `Fix(v) = ∅`, which is finite and reduced — so the
empty case is not a counterexample, and a prover should not spend effort
excluding it.)  For reducedness: in characteristic `0` a nonzero isogeny is
étale, so that fibre is reduced.  The check that would refute it: a `v` with
`v ≫ d.f = d.f`, additive, satisfying `hgeom`, whose fixed locus is
positive-dimensional or non-reduced.

**A CHEAPER ROUTE TO THAT REDUCEDNESS, and a prover should try it FIRST — the
trade it describes has been TAKEN, and `_hadd` above IS its hypothesis.**  The
`τ_Q ∘ ψ` classification of endomorphisms of an elliptic curve is NOT in this
tree and is a substantial piece of work.  It is also not what the only call site
needs: there `v` is the underlying morphism of `2Ψ` with `Ψ` carrying
