**And the finiteness that Cartier needs is itself an obligation**, worth naming
so it is not discovered late: `ker (v − 1)` is finite over the base only when
`v − 1` is a nonzero isogeny, which is the same degenerate case the next
paragraph rules out — so the two obligations are one, not two.

**THE DEGENERATE CASE IS RULED OUT BY `hgeom` AND `geom_cyclic` TOGETHER, and a
prover must not skip it.**  If `ψ = 1` then `Fix(v)` is `∅` (fine — the
conclusion is then vacuous on points and holds) or all of `d.E` (when `v = 𝟙`).
In the second case `hgeom` says every `ℚ̄`-point of `d.E` lies in `C`, while
