The proof is three moves and no geometry:

1. **`Fix(v)` is a SCHEME, namely `equalizer v (𝟙 d.E)`.**  `Scheme` has all
   finite limits (`AlgebraicGeometry`'s `HasFiniteLimits Scheme`, from a terminal
   object and pullbacks), so the equaliser exists with its full API; no
   separatedness of `d.E` and no construction of an ideal sheaf is needed, which
   is what the old docstring's "closed subscheme" phrasing would have cost.
2. **The arbitrary test scheme `W` disappears immediately.**  `hu : u ≫ v = u` is
   `u ≫ v = u ≫ 𝟙`, so `equalizer.lift u` factors `u` through `Fix(v)`, and it
   suffices to factor the SINGLE morphism `equalizer.ι` through `d.cyc.ι`.  This
   is the step that makes the leaf below a statement about one explicit scheme
   rather than about a family.
3. **The descent from `ℚ̄`-points is
   `exists_factor_of_geomSections_of_isReduced_of_isFinite`** above, applied to
   `π := equalizer.ι ≫ d.f`: a section `w` of `π` gives the `ℚ̄`-point
   `w ≫ equalizer.ι` of `d.E`, which is fixed by `v` by `equalizer.condition`,
   so `hgeom` factors it through `d.cyc.ι`.

**WHAT IS LEFT, and it is exactly the paragraph above about `τ_Q ∘ ψ` and
Cartier**: that `Fix(v)` is FINITE and REDUCED over `Spec ℚ̄`.  That is the leaf
above, and the additivity hypothesis is carried precisely to make Cartier's
theorem available for it.

**`hgeom` IS LOAD-BEARING TWICE**, and the second time is easy to miss: besides
supplying the containment on points in step 3, it is what rules out `v = 𝟙`, and
at `v = 𝟙` the leaf above is FALSE — `Fix(𝟙) = d.E`, which is not finite over the
base.  So the leaf above could not have been stated with `hgeom` dropped, however
much cleaner that would read. -/
theorem exists_factor_of_comp_eq_of_geomFixed {N : ℕ}
    (d : Gamma0Datum N (Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))))
    (v : d.E ⟶ d.E) (hv : v ≫ d.f = d.f)
    (hadd : IsAdditiveOn d.ab d.ab v hv)
    (hgeom : ∀ x : RelPoint d.f (𝟙 (Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)))),
      x.1 ≫ v = x.1 → RelPoint.LiesIn d.cyc.ι x)
    {W : Scheme.{0}} (u : W ⟶ d.E) (hu : u ≫ v = u) :
    ∃ w : W ⟶ d.cyc.C, w ≫ d.cyc.ι = u := by
  obtain ⟨hfin, hred⟩ := isFinite_isReduced_fixedLocus_of_geomFixed d v hv hadd hgeom
  haveI := hfin
  haveI := hred
  have hcond : equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ v = equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) := by
    simpa using equalizer.condition v (𝟙 d.E)
  have hsec : ∀ w : Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)) ⟶ equalizer v (𝟙 d.E),
      w ≫ (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) = 𝟙 _ →
      ∃ y : Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)) ⟶ d.cyc.C,
        y ≫ d.cyc.ι = w ≫ equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) := by
    intro w hw
    exact hgeom ⟨w ≫ equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E), by rw [Category.assoc]; exact hw⟩
      (by rw [Category.assoc, hcond])
  obtain ⟨b, hb⟩ := exists_factor_of_geomSections_of_isReduced_of_isFinite
    (K := AlgebraicClosure ℚ) (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E) ≫ d.f) (equalizer.ι v (𝟙 d.E))
    d.cyc.ι hsec
  refine ⟨equalizer.lift u (by rw [hu, Category.comp_id]) ≫ b, ?_⟩
  rw [Category.assoc, hb, equalizer.lift_ι]

open _root_.CategoryTheory _root_.AlgebraicGeometry _root_.Fermat in
/-- **LEAF 2 (cut 2026-07-30): a level structure that agrees with the fixed locus
of `v` on `ℚ̄`-POINTS agrees with it at EVERY test scheme.**

TRUE, and it is a statement about two CLOSED SUBSCHEMES of an elliptic curve
over an algebraically closed field of characteristic `0`.  Both sides of `hgeom`
are factorisation conditions through closed subschemes of `d.E`:

* `RelPoint.LiesIn d.cyc.ι x` is "`x.1` factors through `d.cyc.ι`", and
  `d.cyc.isClosedImmersion` makes that factorisation unique when it exists;
* `x.1 ≫ v = x.1` is "`x.1` factors through the equaliser of `v` and `𝟙`", a
  closed subscheme because `d.E` is separated (`d.ab.proper` over the affine
  `Spec ℚ̄`).

So the conclusion is exactly the statement that those two closed subschemes are
EQUAL, and the hypothesis is that they have the same `ℚ̄`-points.  Over an
algebraically closed field that upgrade needs both to be REDUCED, and both are:

* `d.cyc.C` is finite (`isFinite`) and flat (`flat`) over `Spec ℚ̄` and its
  relative points form a subgroup (`zero_liesIn`, `add_liesIn`, `neg_liesIn`),
  which it REPRESENTS since `ι` is a closed immersion — so it is a finite flat
  group scheme over a field of characteristic `0`, hence étale by Cartier's
  theorem (Waterhouse, *Introduction to Affine Group Schemes*, Thm. 11.4), hence
  reduced.  This is the same argument `CyclicSubgroupOfOrder`'s own docstring
  gives for why `geom_cyclic` may be phrased pointwise;
* the fixed locus of `v` is reduced too, and here is the reason, which is
  specific to curves: a morphism `v` of the elliptic curve `d.E` over the base is
  `τ_Q ∘ ψ` for an isogeny `ψ` and a translation `τ_Q`, so `Fix(v)` is the fibre
  of the isogeny `ψ − 1` over `−Q`; in characteristic `0` a nonzero isogeny is
  étale, so that fibre is reduced.  The degenerate case `ψ = 1` gives `Fix(v) = ∅`
  or `Fix(v) = d.E`, and `hgeom` together with `d.cyc.geom_cyclic` (which pins
  the number of `ℚ̄`-points of `C` to `N`) rules the second out.

**`hv` IS LOAD-BEARING and the leaf is FALSE without it.**  A bare morphism of
schemes `d.E ⟶ d.E` need not lie over `Spec ℚ̄`: since `d.E` is proper and
connected, `Γ(d.E) = ℚ̄`, so such a morphism may act on `ℚ̄` by a nontrivial field
automorphism, and then it is not of the form `τ_Q ∘ ψ` and the reducedness
argument above collapses.  `hv` is available for free at the only call site — the
morphism there is the underlying morphism of an element of `RelPoint d.f d.f`,
whose defining property IS `hv`.

**CHARACTERISTIC `0` IS LOAD-BEARING on both halves**, which is why the base is
spelled `Spec ℚ̄` rather than an arbitrary algebraically closed field: in
characteristic `ℓ` the group scheme `μ_ℓ` is finite flat and NOT reduced, and
`Fix(Frobenius)` is not reduced either, so a leaf stated over a general base
would be false.

**LEVEL-GENERIC**: `N` is unconstrained and no Weierstrass model appears.  The
two sibling leaves `WeierstrassCurve.nonempty_isNIsogenyPair_of_gamma0Model` and
`WeierstrassCurve.exists_isogenyIsom_of_gamma0Model_isBaseChangeOf` need exactly
this descent; they should cite it rather than rebuild it.

**SPLIT AND PROVEN 2026-07-30** over the two halves below,
`iota_comp_eq_of_geomLiesIn` (`C ⊆ Fix(v)`) and
`exists_factor_of_comp_eq_of_geomFixed` (`Fix(v) ⊆ C`).  Two observations made
the split worth doing, and both are recorded here because they are what the
next owner needs and neither is visible from the statement:

1. **The `⟶` direction of this iff carries no descent at all — it is ONE
   equation of morphisms.**  `RelPoint.LiesIn ι x` is by definition
   `∃ y, y ≫ ι = x.1`, so `x.1 ≫ v = (y ≫ ι) ≫ v = y ≫ (ι ≫ v)`, and the
   whole direction, at every test scheme simultaneously, follows from
   `ι ≫ v = ι`.  That is `iota_comp_eq_of_geomLiesIn`, and it needs only `C`
   reduced — the *easy* half of the reducedness discussion above, since `C` is
   finite over `Spec ℚ̄` and Cartier's theorem is applied to it alone.  The
   `Fix(v)` reducedness argument — the `τ_Q ∘ ψ` decomposition, which is the
   part with real content — is confined to the other half.
2. **The base point is inert.**  `RelPoint.LiesIn` does not mention `g`, and
   `x.1 ≫ v = x.1` does not mention `g` either; conversely every
   `u : W ⟶ d.E` IS a relative point, over its own induced base point
   `u ≫ d.f`.  So the `RelPoint`-shaped statement and the base-point-free
   statement about morphisms into `d.E` are literally equivalent, and the
   second half is therefore stated base-point-free.  The two sibling leaves
   should cite whichever shape they meet.

**`hadd` ADDED 2026-07-30 (flt-lean-292), and it is not a weakening of the
statement's reach.**  `exists_factor_of_comp_eq_of_geomFixed` now takes `v`
additive — the trade its own docstring recommended, which turns the missing
`τ_Q ∘ ψ` theory into Cartier's theorem, already on disk — so this assembly
carries the hypothesis through.  It is FREE at every call site that can exist:
`v` here is always `2Ψ` for a homomorphism `Ψ`, and `isAdditiveOn_twoPost`
discharges it in one application.  The two sibling leaves
`WeierstrassCurve.nonempty_isNIsogenyPair_of_gamma0Model` and
`WeierstrassCurve.exists_isogenyIsom_of_gamma0Model_isBaseChangeOf` meet `v` in
that same shape and so pay nothing either. -/
theorem liesIn_iff_of_geomFibre_of_fixedLocus {N : ℕ}
    (d : Gamma0Datum N (Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))))
    (v : d.E ⟶ d.E) (hv : v ≫ d.f = d.f)
    (hadd : IsAdditiveOn d.ab d.ab v hv)
    (hgeom : ∀ x : RelPoint d.f (𝟙 (Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)))),
      RelPoint.LiesIn d.cyc.ι x ↔ x.1 ≫ v = x.1)
    {T' : Scheme.{0}} {g : T' ⟶ Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))}
    (x : RelPoint d.f g) :
    RelPoint.LiesIn d.cyc.ι x ↔ x.1 ≫ v = x.1 := by
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨y, hy⟩
    rw [← hy, Category.assoc,
      iota_comp_eq_of_geomLiesIn d v hv fun z hz => (hgeom z).mp hz]
  · intro hx
    exact exists_factor_of_comp_eq_of_geomFixed d v hv hadd
      (fun z hz => (hgeom z).mpr hz) x.1 hx

/-- **MAZUR AT `43, 67, 163`, IN WEIERSTRASS VOCABULARY, OVER THE `X0.lean`
LEAVES** (PROVEN 2026-07-31): a Galois-stable cyclic subgroup of order
`p ∈ {43, 67, 163}` on a curve `E/ℚ` is the kernel of `√−p`, for an
endomorphism `φ = (1 + √−p)/2` of `E_ℚ̄` satisfying `φ² + (p+1)/4 = φ`.

**This is `exists_endMinpoly_of_stable_cyclic_mazurLevel`, restated ABOVE the
`ℚ̄`-moduli leaf and proven a completely different way.**  The declaration below
of that name reaches the same conclusion through Serre's isogeny-signature
pipeline and the Atkin–Lehner/Vélu packaging; this one goes straight to
`X0.lean`'s statement of Mazur's Theorem 1 in `j`-invariant form.  It exists
because the moduli-side leaf
`nonempty_isCMByRamifiedMaximalOrder_geomPoint_mazurLevel` is declared between
the two, and Lean's declaration order made the sibling below uncitable from it —
that is the circularity of item (2) of the block docstring, and this is what
breaks it.

**Three ingredients, all upstream in `X0.lean`, none of them new here.**

1. `Fermat.mem_isolatedJInvariants_of_stable_classNumberOne` (LEAF, open): the
   stable cyclic `p`-subgroup forces `E.j ∈ isolatedJInvariants p`.  This is
   Mazur, and it is where the arithmetic of this theorem is charged.
2. `isolatedJInvariants p ⊆ isolatedCMJInvariants` at these three `p`, by
   `decide`: each row is a singleton listed in the CM set.  Not stated as a
   lemma anywhere — it is one tactic call and would have exactly one consumer.
3. `Fermat.exists_cmEndomorphism_of_mem_isolatedCMJInvariants` (LEAF, open) for
   the minimal polynomial, and `Fermat.ker_cmSqrt_eq_zmultiples_of_stable`
   (PROVEN) for the kernel clause `ker (2φ − 1) = ⟨g⟩`.

**The kernel clause is the one that was believed missing**, and the docstring on
the leaf below this one recorded it as arithmetic that "would have to become a
named leaf".  It was already proven, inside the body of
`not_twoStableLines_of_cmEndomorphism`, and hoisting it out cost nothing — see
that theorem's new docstring in `X0.lean`.

**The packaging step is not vacuous either.**  `exists_cmEndomorphism_…` hands
`φ` over as a bare `AddMonoidHom` with an `IsIsogeny` certificate beside it; the
consumer here wants an element of the RING `WeierstrassCurve.End`, whose
membership predicate IS that certificate, and whose integer casts act as
multiplication on points DEFINITIONALLY (`End.intCast_apply`).  So `⟨f, hfiso⟩`
is the whole of it, `2 * φ − 1` coincides with `X0.lean`'s `cmSqrt E f` on the
nose, and the two conjuncts transfer by `rfl`-level reasoning.  That is what
makes this theorem twenty lines rather than a translation layer.

**hp IS LOAD-BEARING**, for the same reason as on every sibling: without it the
statement claims that a rational cyclic `N`-isogeny forces CM by the maximal
order of discriminant `−N`, which is false at small `N` and false at `N = 37`. -/
theorem exists_endMinpoly_of_stable_cyclic_isolatedJ
    (E : WeierstrassCurve ℚ) [E.IsElliptic] {p : ℕ}
    (hp : p ∈ ({43, 67, 163} : Finset ℕ))
    (g : (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).Point) (hg : addOrderOf g = p)
    (hstable : ∀ σ : Field.absoluteGaloisGroup ℚ, ∀ x ∈ AddSubgroup.zmultiples g,
      WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.map
        (σ : AlgebraicClosure ℚ ≃ₐ[ℚ] AlgebraicClosure ℚ).toAlgHom x ∈
        AddSubgroup.zmultiples g) :
    ∃ φ : WeierstrassCurve.End (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).toAffine,
      φ * φ + (((p + 1) / 4 : ℕ) :
          WeierstrassCurve.End (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).toAffine) = φ ∧
        AddMonoidHom.ker
            (((2 * φ - 1 : WeierstrassCurve.End (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).toAffine) :
                AddMonoid.End (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).toAffine.Point) :
              (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).Point →+ (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).Point)
          = AddSubgroup.zmultiples g := by
  have hp5 : p ∈ ({11, 19, 43, 67, 163} : Finset ℕ) := by fin_cases hp <;> decide
  -- **1.** Mazur's theorem, in `X0.lean`'s `j`-invariant vocabulary.
  have hj : E.j ∈ Fermat.isolatedJInvariants p :=
    Fermat.mem_isolatedJInvariants_of_stable_classNumberOne hp E g hg hstable
  have hsub : Fermat.isolatedJInvariants p ⊆ Fermat.isolatedCMJInvariants := by
    fin_cases hp <;> decide
  have hjcm : E.j ∈ Fermat.isolatedJInvariants p ∩ Fermat.isolatedCMJInvariants :=
    Finset.mem_inter.mpr ⟨hj, hsub hj⟩
  -- **2.** the CM endomorphism `φ = (1 + √−p)/2`, as a bare `AddMonoidHom`.
  obtain ⟨f, hfiso, hfsq⟩ :=
    Fermat.exists_cmEndomorphism_of_mem_isolatedCMJInvariants hp5 E hjcm
  -- **3.** its kernel clause: the stable line IS `ker √−p`.
  have hker := Fermat.ker_cmSqrt_eq_zmultiples_of_stable hp5 E f hfiso hfsq g hg hstable
  -- **4.** package `f` as an element of the endomorphism RING; both conjuncts
  -- are then definitional.
  refine ⟨⟨(f : AddMonoid.End (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).toAffine.Point), hfiso⟩, ?_, ?_⟩
  · refine Subtype.ext (AddMonoidHom.ext fun P => ?_)
    show f (f P) + (((p + 1) / 4 : ℕ) • P) = f P
    exact hfsq P
  · rw [← hker]
    congr 1

open _root_.CategoryTheory _root_.AlgebraicGeometry _root_.Fermat in
/-- **LEAF 1 (cut 2026-07-30): the point-level CM endomorphism of the Weierstrass
model becomes an actual SCHEME endomorphism of the datum.**

This is the geometric residue of the bridge below, and the only part of it that
is geometry.  What it has to do, in the order a prover meets it:

1. `hmodel` gives an open immersion `ι : weierstrassAffine E ⟶ d₀.E` over
   `Spec ℚ` whose range is the complement of the zero section, and
   `exists_affineChart_projModel` gives the matching chart of
   `WeierstrassCurve.Projective.proj E`; `Fermat.exists_isIso_of_affineChart`
   (PROVEN, `ModularCurve/EllipticScheme.lean`) turns the pair into an
   ISOMORPHISM `proj E ≅ d₀.E` over `Spec ℚ`.  All three are proven —
   in particular `smoothOfRelativeDimension_one_of_affineChart`, which the
   docstring below still describes as the one open leaf under this route, is
   PROVEN as of release 6, so no restatement of
   `exists_hom_symm_of_affineChart` is needed;
2. `φ` carries `IsIsogeny`, hence `IsRationalMap`: polynomials `A B C D E` over
   `ℚ̄` computing the coordinates of `φ P` from those of `P` away from `Z(B) ∪
   Z(E)`.  That is a morphism out of a dense open of the affine chart, and
   `AlgebraicGeometry.exists_unique_extension_of_isSmoothProperCurve`
   (`Mathlib/AlgebraicGeometry/CurveExtension.lean`, PROVEN) extends it across
   that finite bad locus into the PROPER target;
3. `bc` transports the result from `Spec ℚ` to `Spec ℚ̄`;
4. additivity is `Fermat.isAdditiveOn_of_post_zero` (rigidity: a morphism of
   abelian schemes fixing the zero section is a homomorphism), and the two
   remaining conjuncts are the minimal polynomial and the level structure, both
   asked for only at the `ℚ̄`-fibre / the tautological point thanks to
   `relPoint_post_sq_of_self` and `relPoint_post_add_post_eq_iff` above.

**ALGEBRAICITY IS IN THE STATEMENT, and it must stay there.**  `φ` is a
`WeierstrassCurve.End`, which carries `IsIsogeny` and hence `IsRationalMap`; the
leaf would be FALSE with `φ` weakened to an additive endomorphism of the point
group.  As an abstract group `E(ℚ̄) ≅ (ℚ/ℤ)² ⊕ ⊕_𝔠 ℚ`, so its free part is a
`ℚ`-vector space of continuum dimension and carries `ℚ`-linear endomorphisms with
minimal polynomial `X² − X + (p+1)/4` — irreducible, its discriminant being `−p`
— that are nowhere near algebraic.

**`hp` IS LOAD-BEARING**; see the subsection note above for the witness and for
why the `α`-ambiguity in `e` makes it so.  Briefly: the conclusion does not
mention `e`, so the ambiguity cannot falsify the leaf, but it does leave `d₀.cyc`
pinned only as *some* Galois-stable subgroup of order `p`, and it is `hp` —
through the uniqueness of that subgroup on a CM curve at `43, 67, 163` — that
identifies it with `E[𝔭] = ker (2φ − 1)`.  At `p = 3, 7, 11, 19`, the other
class-number-one values with `p ≡ 3 mod 4`, the hypotheses are still satisfiable
and the identification fails.

**PROVEN 2026-07-31 over the single leaf `exists_relSchemeEnd_geomEquiv_of_weierstrassModel`
immediately above** — which is the GEOMETRY, level-generic and `hp`-free.  Three
corrections to the paragraphs above, all found by writing the assembly.

1. **The uniqueness of the Galois-stable order-`p` subgroup is NOT needed, and a
   prover must not go looking for it.**  The paragraph above prescribes
   Mazur-level input ("at `43, 67, 163` the Galois-stable subgroup of order `p`
   on such a curve is UNIQUE"), which would have been close to circular here.
   The assembly does not use it.  What it does instead is apply the ALREADY
   PROVEN `exists_endMinpoly_of_stable_cyclic_isolatedJ` — which is exactly
   where `hp` lives, and is where the arithmetic already was — to the generator
   of `d₀.cyc`'s own geometric fibre, pulled back along `ε₀`.  That produces a
   SECOND root `φ'` of `X² − X + (p+1)/4` in `End(E⁄ℚ̄)` whose `ker (2φ' − 1)`
   IS the level structure, by construction.  It then only remains to see that
   the two roots have the same kernel, which is formalism:
   `(φ − φ')(φ + φ' − 1) = (φ² − φ) − (φ'² − φ') = 0` — this is where
   `End.mul_comm_charZero` is consumed — and `End W` has no zero divisors
   (`MazurEndLattice.end_mul_ne_zero`), so `φ = φ'` or `φ = 1 − φ'`; in the
   second case `2φ − 1 = −(2φ' − 1)`, same kernel.  **General lesson: when a
   docstring says a leaf needs the UNIQUENESS of an object, check first whether
   the theorem that PRODUCES the object can simply be applied to the rival, and
   the two compared inside the ring.**

2. **`e`, `he` and `hliesIn` are therefore NOT USED**, and are kept only so that
   the signature — and the call site 400 lines below — does not move.  They are
   redundant: `d₀.cyc.geom_cyclic` supplies the generator directly, and `ε₀`
   (produced by LEAF 1a) supplies the Galois-equivariant identification, so the
   caller's `e` never has to be compared with the one the model induces.  That
   is what dissolves the `α`-ambiguity the subsection note above worries about:
   the assembly simply never mentions the ambiguous `e`.  A successor is free to
   delete the three binders and the matching arguments at the call site.

3. So the whole of what remains is LEAF 1a, and it carries no arithmetic.

**NOT VACUOUS**: `hmodel`, `e` and `he` are jointly satisfiable — that is
`Fermat.exists_weierstrassModel_geomFibreAddEquiv_of_gamma0Datum`, PROVEN — and
`φ` with `hsq`/`hliesIn` is satisfiable by
`exists_endMinpoly_of_stable_cyclic_isolatedJ`, also proven above, so the whole
hypothesis list is realised at each of the three levels by the CM curve carrying
its ramified `p`-isogeny. -/
theorem exists_relSchemeEnd_of_endMinpoly_of_weierstrassModel (p : ℕ)
    (hp : p ∈ ({43, 67, 163} : Finset ℕ))
    {d₀ : Gamma0Datum p SpecQ}
    {d : Gamma0Datum p (Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)))}
    (bc : IsBaseChangeOf (specAlgClos ℚ) d d₀)
    (E : WeierstrassCurve ℚ) [E.IsElliptic]
    (hmodel : IsWeierstrassModel d₀.ab E)
    (_e : letI := d₀.ab.addCommGroup (specAlgClos ℚ ≫ 𝟙 SpecQ)
      (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).Point ≃+ GeomFibrePt d₀.f (𝟙 SpecQ))
    (_he : letI := d₀.ab.addCommGroup (specAlgClos ℚ ≫ 𝟙 SpecQ)
      ∀ (σ : Field.absoluteGaloisGroup ℚ) (x : (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).Point),
        _e (WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.map
            (σ : AlgebraicClosure ℚ ≃ₐ[ℚ] AlgebraicClosure ℚ).toAlgHom x)
          = d₀.ab.galSMul (𝟙 SpecQ) σ (_e x))
    (φ : WeierstrassCurve.End (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).toAffine)
    (hsq : φ * φ + (((p + 1) / 4 : ℕ) :
        WeierstrassCurve.End (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).toAffine) = φ)
    (_hliesIn : letI := d₀.ab.addCommGroup (specAlgClos ℚ ≫ 𝟙 SpecQ)
      ∀ x : (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).Point,
        RelPoint.LiesIn d₀.cyc.ι (_e x) ↔
          x ∈ AddMonoidHom.ker
            (((2 * φ - 1 : WeierstrassCurve.End (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).toAffine) :
                AddMonoid.End (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).toAffine.Point) :
              (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).Point →+ (E⁄(AlgebraicClosure ℚ)).Point)) :
    ∃ (Ψ : d.E ⟶ d.E) (hΨ : Ψ ≫ d.f = d.f),
      IsAdditiveOn d.ab d.ab Ψ hΨ ∧
      (∀ x : RelPoint d.f (𝟙 (Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)))),
        RelPoint.LiesIn d.cyc.ι x ↔
          x.1 ≫ (d.ab.add (RelPoint.post Ψ hΨ (RelPoint.self d.f))
            (RelPoint.post Ψ hΨ (RelPoint.self d.f))).1 = x.1) ∧
      (letI := d.ab.addCommGroup d.f;
        d.ab.add (RelPoint.post Ψ hΨ (RelPoint.post Ψ hΨ (RelPoint.self d.f)))
            (((p + 1) / 4 : ℕ) • RelPoint.self d.f)
          = RelPoint.post Ψ hΨ (RelPoint.self d.f)) := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨Ψ, hΨ, hadd, hpt, hmin⟩ :=
    FixedLocusOfAdditive.exists_relSchemeEnd_geomEquiv_of_weierstrassModel
      ((p + 1) / 4) bc E hmodel φ hsq
  refine ⟨Ψ, hΨ, hadd, ?_, hmin⟩
  letI := d.ab.addCommGroup (𝟙 (Spec (CommRingCat.of (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))))
  letI := d₀.ab.addCommGroup (specAlgClos ℚ ≫ 𝟙 SpecQ)
  obtain ⟨ε, ε₀, hlink, hgal, hint⟩ := hpt
  -- **1.** the generator of `d₀`'s level structure, pulled back to `E(ℚ̄)` along `ε₀`
  obtain ⟨y, -, hyOrd, hyGen⟩ :=
    d₀.cyc.geom_cyclic (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) (specAlgClos ℚ ≫ 𝟙 SpecQ)
  have hord : addOrderOf (ε₀.symm y) = p := by
    rw [AddEquiv.addOrderOf_eq]; exact hyOrd
  have hstab : ∀ σ : Field.absoluteGaloisGroup ℚ,
      ∀ x ∈ AddSubgroup.zmultiples (ε₀.symm y),
        WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.map
          (σ : AlgebraicClosure ℚ ≃ₐ[ℚ] AlgebraicClosure ℚ).toAlgHom x ∈
          AddSubgroup.zmultiples (ε₀.symm y) := by
    intro σ x hx
    have hex : ε₀ x ∈ AddSubgroup.zmultiples y := by
      obtain ⟨k, hk⟩ := AddSubgroup.mem_zmultiples_iff.mp hx
      exact AddSubgroup.mem_zmultiples_iff.mpr
        ⟨k, by rw [← hk, map_zsmul, AddEquiv.apply_symm_apply]⟩
    obtain ⟨w, hw⟩ := (hyGen (ε₀ x)).mpr hex
    have hIn : RelPoint.LiesIn d₀.cyc.ι (d₀.ab.galSMul (𝟙 SpecQ) σ (ε₀ x)) := by
      refine ⟨Fermat.specGal σ ≫ w, ?_⟩
      show (Fermat.specGal σ ≫ w) ≫ d₀.cyc.ι = Fermat.specGal σ ≫ (ε₀ x).1
